mes 4.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $P$ and $R$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476067 :question/problem "Which of the following is a $1\\times 3$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nP = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -0.1 & -0.1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nQ = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nR = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $P$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $Q$ has $3$ rows and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 1.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $R$ has $1$ row and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 3.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $R$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476075 :question/problem "Which of the following is a $2\\times 3$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -0.1 \\\\ 1.1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nC = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1 \\\\ -0.1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nD = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -0.1 & -2 \\\\ 5 & \\sqrt{3} & -0.1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $B$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $3$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 2.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $D$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 3.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $D$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476085 :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3\\times 1$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nF = \\begin{bmatrix}1.1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nG = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 1.1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nH = \\begin{bmatrix}4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $F$ has $3$ rows and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 1.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $G$ has $1$ row and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $H$ has $1$ row and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 1.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, only $F$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476094 :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3\\times 3$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nL = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0 \\\\ 7 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nM = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -3 & -0.1 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nN = \\begin{bmatrix}1.1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1.1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $L$ has $3$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 2.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $M$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $N$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $N$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476096 :question/problem "Which of the following is a $4\\times 3$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -0.1 \\\\ -0.1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -0.1 \\\\ -1 & 1.1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nC = \\begin{bmatrix}1.1 & \\sqrt{2} & -0.1 \\\\ -2 & -0.1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $A$ has $4$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $4\\times 2.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $B$ has $4$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $4\\times 3.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476097 :question/problem "Which of the following is a $1\\times 2$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nE = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nF = \\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nG = \\begin{bmatrix}1.1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $E$ has $1$ row and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 1.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $F$ has $2$ rows and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 1.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $G$ has $1$ row and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 2.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $G$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix."}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087654 :question/problem "![](images/9a/9af4801abdec165de2b720a21dd6a727c578e32cbae9c9c54bd589a1b65043b0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three train stations, where the nodes represent stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents the number of routes between the three stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}a}$</td><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$0$</td><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}b}$</td><td>$2$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}c}$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station A and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $A$ to $A,$ and so $a=1.$\n\n- No arrows are starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there are no routes from $B$ to $B,$ and so $b=0.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $C$ to $A,$ and so $c=1.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$0$</td><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td><td>$2$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+0+1 = 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088004 :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbdf5c2470229ae3ba10ee18bccd2dc764407897397466b157474bf2b819f5d9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows bus routes between three stations, where nodes represent the stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents information about the number of routes between each of the three stations. Find the value of $a + b + c$.\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$0$</td><td>$a$</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$0$</td><td>$b$</td><td>$c$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station A and ending at Station B. So, there is only one route from $A$ to $B$, and so $a=1$.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there is only one route from $B$ to $B$, and so $b=1$.\n\n- There are $2$ arrows starting at Station B and ending at Station C. So, there are $2$ routes from $B$ to $C$, and so $c=2$.\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$0$</td><td>$1$</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$0$</td><td>$1$</td><td>$2$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+1+2 = 4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088005 :question/problem "![](images/ab/abbc0e5ce20a52263ba067d1563708f803e33def4f931a3d3badedd1c8ff4908.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three stations, where nodes represent the stations and arrows represent the directed lines between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents information about the number of routes between each of the three stations. Find the value of $a + b + c$.\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n